insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
($q$a2-q-600$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Settings category lets a user switch a PC between signing in with a Microsoft account and signing in with a local account?$q$,$q$["Accounts","Privacy","Apps","System"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Settings > Accounts is where the sign-in method (Microsoft account vs. local account), Windows Hello, family members, and sync options are managed. Privacy governs app permissions, Apps manages installed software, and System covers display/power/storage settings.$q$),
($q$a2-q-601$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A department wants Windows Update to stop restarting PCs automatically between 8 AM and 6 PM on business days. Which Windows Update setting accomplishes this?$q$,$q$["Active hours","Focus assist","Storage Sense","Delivery Optimization"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Active hours (under Settings > Windows Update > Advanced options) tells Windows the period during which the PC is in normal use, so it avoids automatic restarts during that window. Focus assist silences notifications, Storage Sense frees disk space, and Delivery Optimization controls how update files are shared between PCs.$q$),
($q$a2-q-602$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$A technician needs to completely remove an unwanted application from a Windows 11 PC using the Settings app rather than a third-party uninstaller. Which page should they open?$q$,$q$["Settings > Apps > Apps & features","Settings > Privacy & security > App permissions","Settings > System > Storage","Settings > Accounts > Family & other users"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Apps & features lists every installed application and lets a technician uninstall (or modify/repair) it directly. Privacy controls what an app can access, Storage manages disk space, and Family & other users manages who can sign in to the PC.$q$),
($q$a2-q-603$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following are configured from the Privacy (& security) page in Windows Settings? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["Whether a given app can use the camera","Whether a given app can use the microphone","Which browser opens web links by default","How a fixed disk is partitioned"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Camera and microphone access are per-app permissions controlled from Privacy (& security). The default browser is set from Apps > Default apps, and disk partitioning is handled by Disk Management, not Settings.$q$),
($q$a2-q-604$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A laptop user complains that the screen turns off far too quickly while they are reading a long document, even though they are still actively using the mouse. Which Settings page should be adjusted?$q$,$q$["System > Power & battery (sleep/screen timeout)","System > Notifications","Privacy & security > Location","Devices > Touchpad"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Screen and sleep timeouts are configured under System > Power & battery (Power & sleep in older builds), separately for "on battery" and "plugged in." Notifications, location privacy, and touchpad settings have no effect on how long the screen stays awake.$q$),
($q$a2-q-605$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A technician wants to enable inbound Remote Desktop connections to a coworker's desktop PC running Windows 11 Home so they can help remotely. What is the outcome?$q$,$q$["It works immediately after turning on the Remote Desktop toggle under Settings > System","It cannot be done - Windows Home cannot host inbound Remote Desktop sessions regardless of settings","It requires enabling Focus assist first","It requires disabling the local firewall entirely"]$q$::jsonb,1::jsonb,$q$Hosting (accepting) an inbound Remote Desktop connection requires Windows 10/11 Pro or Enterprise; Home edition does not include the Remote Desktop host feature at all, no matter what is toggled in Settings. Home can still act as an RDP client to connect out to a Pro/Enterprise machine.$q$),
($q$a2-q-606$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which Settings page is used to pair a new Bluetooth mouse with a Windows PC?$q$,$q$["Devices > Bluetooth & other devices","System > Display","Apps > Optional features","Accounts > Sign-in options"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Bluetooth & other devices (under Devices) is where new Bluetooth accessories are discovered and paired. Display is for screen settings, Optional features adds Windows components, and Sign-in options configures PINs/biometrics.$q$),
($q$a2-q-607$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for adding a local printer through Windows Settings into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Select the detected printer from the list and choose Add device","Open Settings > Devices > Printers & scanners","Wait for Windows to search for nearby printers","Click Add device / Add a printer or scanner"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$The workflow starts by opening Printers & scanners, clicking Add a printer or scanner to trigger a search, waiting for Windows to detect nearby devices, and finally selecting the correct printer and confirming Add device.$q$),
($q$a2-q-608$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which Settings page shows a PC's installed RAM, processor, Windows edition, and activation status, and also lets you rename the PC?$q$,$q$["System > About","System > Notifications","Apps > Apps & features","Devices > Printers & scanners"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$System > About is the modern replacement for the old Control Panel System page: device specs, Windows edition/version/build, and activation status all appear there, along with the option to rename the device.$q$),
($q$a2-q-609$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$An employee is concerned that Windows is sending more usage and device-health information to Microsoft than necessary. Which setting controls this?$q$,$q$["Diagnostic data, under Privacy & security","Active hours, under Windows Update","Default apps, under Apps","Storage Sense, under System"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$The Diagnostic data setting under Privacy (& security) controls how much telemetry (Required vs. Optional) Windows and apps send to Microsoft. Active hours governs restart timing, Default apps controls file associations, and Storage Sense manages disk cleanup.$q$),
($q$a2-q-610$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each Windows Settings feature to what it actually does.$q$,$q${"left":["Storage Sense","Focus assist","Default apps","Find My Device"],"right":["Chooses which program opens a given type of file","Helps locate a lost or stolen Windows device","Automatically frees disk space by deleting temporary files","Silences notifications during set times or activities"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Storage Sense automatically clears out temporary files to reclaim disk space; Focus assist silences notifications during chosen times or activities; Default apps decides which program opens a given file type; and Find My Device helps locate a lost or stolen PC, provided it was enabled beforehand.$q$),
($q$a2-q-611$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A user previously denied a video-calling app access to the microphone and now needs to allow it. Where does a technician re-enable that access?$q$,$q$["Privacy & security > Microphone, then allow the specific app","Apps > Startup","System > Sound output device list","Devices > Bluetooth & other devices"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Per-app microphone access is controlled from Privacy & security > Microphone, where both a global microphone toggle and a per-app list of allowed apps live. Startup, sound output, and Bluetooth pages do not control microphone permissions.$q$),
($q$a2-q-612$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What is the practical effect of marking a Wi-Fi connection as "metered" in Network & internet settings?$q$,$q$["Windows limits background data usage, pausing many automatic downloads such as some updates and app syncs","The PC is permanently disconnected from that network","File and printer sharing is automatically enabled","The network is automatically reclassified as a Private network"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Marking a connection as metered tells Windows to treat bandwidth as limited, so it pauses or reduces background traffic like Windows Update downloads, OneDrive sync, and some app updates. It does not disconnect the PC, and it has no direct effect on sharing or the network-profile (Public/Private) classification.$q$),
($q$a2-q-613$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$From Settings > Apps > Apps & features, which actions are available for a selected installed application? (Select three.)$q$,$q$["Uninstall the application","Modify or repair the installation, when the installer supports it","Move the application to a different drive, when supported","Change the application file's NTFS permissions directly"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Apps & features exposes uninstall, and for supporting installers, modify/repair and moving the app to another drive. NTFS permissions are changed from a file or folder's Properties > Security tab, not from this page.$q$),
($q$a2-q-614$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A vendor's line-of-business application lists "64-bit Windows 10/11 required" as a system requirement. The target PC is currently running 32-bit Windows 10. What must happen before the application can be installed?$q$,$q$["Add more RAM to the current 32-bit Windows installation","Reinstall the PC with a 64-bit edition of Windows first","Enable hardware virtualization in the BIOS/UEFI","Increase the paging file size to compensate"]$q$::jsonb,1::jsonb,$q$32-bit Windows cannot execute 64-bit binaries at all - the reverse works via WOW64, but not this direction - so the operating system itself must be reinstalled as 64-bit before this application's stated requirement can be met. More RAM, virtualization, and a larger paging file do not change that fundamental limitation.$q$),
($q$a2-q-615$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which installer type natively supports a fully unattended, silent installation (for example, using a /quiet switch) and can later repair itself from that same package?$q$,$q$["A portable executable that requires no installation step","A Windows Installer (.msi) package","A batch script that copies files manually","A disk image (.iso) that must be mounted and browsed manually"]$q$::jsonb,1::jsonb,$q$The Windows Installer format (.msi) is a structured, transactional package that supports silent install/uninstall switches and a built-in repair action from the same file. Portable executables, manual batch copies, and browsing a mounted ISO don't offer that same standardized silent/repair behavior.$q$),
($q$a2-q-616$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$An administrator wants to use Group Policy Software Installation to silently push the same application to every computer in an OU overnight, with no user interaction required. Which installer format does this deployment method require?$q$,$q$["A .msi (Windows Installer) package","A self-extracting .exe built with a third-party installer tool","A .bat script that a user must double-click","A .dmg disk image"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$GPO Software Installation is built around the Windows Installer format and can silently install, upgrade, or remove .msi packages with no user interaction. Generic wrapped .exe installers, scripts needing a manual double-click, and macOS .dmg images aren't natively supported by this deployment method.$q$),
($q$a2-q-617$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following are legitimate distribution methods for deploying an approved application across a managed fleet of company Windows PCs? (Select all that apply.)$q$,$q$["Centralized push deployment via SCCM or Intune","Placing the installer on an internal network share reachable by the target PCs","Installing directly from a pop-up ad promising a \"necessary system update\"","Distributing the application through the Microsoft Store"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$SCCM/Intune, an internal network share, and the Microsoft Store are all controlled, verifiable sources for enterprise software. A pop-up ad promising an urgent "update" is a classic malware/PUP delivery vector, not a legitimate distribution channel, no matter how official it looks.$q$),
($q$a2-q-618$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Why should a technician check an application's stated system requirements before attempting to install it?$q$,$q$["To confirm the target PC's OS version, architecture, and available resources can actually run the software correctly","To decide what color scheme the application will use by default","To choose which desktop wallpaper to set afterward","Because published system requirements only apply to video games, not business software"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$System requirements describe the minimum OS version/architecture, RAM, CPU, disk space, and any required runtime dependencies the software needs to install and run correctly - skipping this check risks a failed or unstable install. Every category of software publishes requirements, not just games.$q$),
($q$a2-q-619$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these steps for deploying an .msi application through Group Policy Software Installation into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Add the .msi as a new package under Computer Configuration > Software Installation in the GPO","Copy the .msi installer to a network share that target computers can reach","Wait for the targeted computers to apply the updated policy (on restart or gpupdate) and install the software","Create a new GPO and link it to the OU containing the target computers"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$The installer must first be reachable on a network share, then a GPO is created and linked to the target OU, then the .msi package is added under that GPO's Software Installation node, and finally the targeted computers apply the policy and run the install.$q$),
($q$a2-q-620$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A line-of-business application built for an older version of Windows displays garbled graphics and crashes on a current Windows 11 PC, even though the vendor confirms the hardware is more than capable. Which built-in feature should be tried first?$q$,$q$["Compatibility mode, configured from the application shortcut or executable's Properties","Focus assist","Storage Sense","BitLocker"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Compatibility mode lets Windows emulate an older Windows version's environment and display settings for a specific application - exactly the kind of legacy-software issue described. Focus assist manages notifications, Storage Sense manages disk space, and BitLocker encrypts drives; none address application compatibility.$q$),
($q$a2-q-621$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$On a 64-bit installation of Windows, where does the installer for a 32-bit application place its files by default?$q$,$q$["C:\\Program Files","C:\\Program Files (x86)","C:\\Windows\\System32","C:\\Users\\Public"]$q$::jsonb,1::jsonb,$q$Sixty-four-bit Windows keeps 32-bit applications in the separate Program Files (x86) folder (with matching registry redirection), apart from native 64-bit applications in Program Files. System32 holds system binaries, and Users\Public is a shared profile location, not an application install target.$q$),
($q$a2-q-622$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A standard (non-administrator) user attempts to install a new application and receives an error indicating administrative privileges are required. What is the direct fix?$q$,$q$["Run the installer elevated - Run as administrator, approved via a UAC prompt with admin credentials","Restart the PC in Safe Mode and try again","Disable the network adapter during installation","Rename the installer's file extension"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Most installers write to protected locations such as Program Files and HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE, which require administrative rights. Running the installer elevated - or having an administrator approve the UAC prompt - grants the access it needs. Safe Mode, disabling networking, and renaming the extension don't address the underlying permissions requirement.$q$),
($q$a2-q-623$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$In the context of installing an application, what is a "dependency"?$q$,$q$["A separate component or runtime the application requires to already be present in order to install or run correctly, such as the .NET Framework or a specific Java version","A user account that is not permitted to run the application","The application's uninstall log file","A backup copy of the application's configuration file"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A dependency is a prerequisite piece of software - a runtime, library, or framework - that the application relies on. If a required dependency is missing, installation can fail outright, or the application can install but fail to run correctly.$q$),
($q$a2-q-624$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each software distribution method to the trait that best describes it.$q$,$q${"left":["Microsoft Store (MSIX/UWP) app","Centralized deployment via SCCM/Intune","Portable application","Installer served from a network share"],"right":["Runs directly with no formal installation step, often from a folder or removable drive","Requires the client PC to reach a shared folder over the LAN to run the installer","Sandboxed and set to update automatically, without a traditional standalone installer","Pushes and tracks the install across many managed devices centrally, without user action"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Store apps (MSIX/UWP) are sandboxed and auto-update without a classic installer; SCCM/Intune push installs centrally and track compliance across many devices without user action; a portable app runs directly with no installation step at all; and a network-share installer still requires the client to reach that share over the LAN and run a normal setup process.$q$),
($q$a2-q-625$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A deployed .msi installation is failing on some machines with no visible error shown to the end user. Which command captures a detailed installation log for troubleshooting?$q$,$q$["msiexec /i app.msi /l*v install.log","msiexec /x app.msi","msiexec /uninstall app.msi","msiexec /repair app.msi with no logging switch"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$The /l*v switch, combined with /i to install, writes a verbose log of every step msiexec takes - the standard first artifact reviewed when a silent .msi deployment fails without a user-visible error. /x uninstalls, and neither remaining option produces a log.$q$),
($q$a2-q-626$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$Before deploying a new business application to every workstation in the office, which of the following should be verified as part of its system requirements? (Select three.)$q$,$q$["Minimum CPU, RAM, and free disk space","The supported Windows version and architecture (32-bit vs. 64-bit)","Any required runtime dependencies, such as .NET Framework or a specific Java version","The desktop wallpaper currently configured on each PC"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$System requirements cover hardware minimums (CPU/RAM/disk), the supported OS version and architecture, and any runtime dependencies the app needs to install or function. A PC's wallpaper has no bearing on whether the application will install or run.$q$),
($q$a2-q-627$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which file extension identifies a Windows Installer package?$q$,$q$[".exe",".msi",".bat",".dmg"]$q$::jsonb,1::jsonb,$q$.msi identifies a Windows Installer package - a structured format supporting silent installation, repair, and uninstall through the same file. .exe is a generic executable that may or may not be an installer, .bat is a script, and .dmg is a macOS disk image, unrelated to Windows installers.$q$),
($q$a2-q-628$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$A technician runs ipconfig on a wired desktop and sees an IP address of 169.254.12.87, with no devices reachable except others on the same subnet. What does this address indicate?$q$,$q$["A correctly assigned static IP from the network administrator","An APIPA address, meaning the PC could not reach a DHCP server","A public IP address issued directly by the ISP","An address reserved for VPN tunnel traffic"]$q$::jsonb,1::jsonb,$q$Addresses in the 169.254.0.0/16 range are Automatic Private IP Addressing (APIPA) - self-assigned by Windows only when no DHCP server responded. Devices with APIPA addresses can only talk to each other on the same local segment, not to the real gateway-issued network or the internet.$q$),
($q$a2-q-629$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$A PC has a stale IP configuration after a network change. Put these ipconfig-based troubleshooting steps in the correct order.$q$,$q$["Verify the newly assigned configuration with ipconfig /all","Release the current DHCP lease with ipconfig /release","Request a new DHCP lease with ipconfig /renew"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Release the stale lease first, request a fresh one from the DHCP server, then confirm the new address, subnet mask, and gateway actually took effect with ipconfig /all. Verifying before renewing would only show the same stale or absent information.$q$),
($q$a2-q-630$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Users can successfully ping each other by IP address on the LAN and can even ping 8.8.8.8 directly, but typing any website name in a browser never resolves. Which setting is most likely misconfigured?$q$,$q$["The subnet mask","The default gateway","The DNS server address","The MAC address of the network adapter"]$q$::jsonb,2::jsonb,$q$Since IP-layer connectivity works both locally and out to a known internet address, the problem is isolated to name resolution - pointing at a missing, unreachable, or misconfigured DNS server. Subnet mask and gateway problems would break IP connectivity itself, and the MAC address plays no role in resolution.$q$),
($q$a2-q-631$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A new employee's laptop joins the office Wi-Fi and Windows automatically classifies the connection as Public, which hides the PC from coworkers' computers even though file sharing with them is required. What should be changed?$q$,$q$["Set the network profile to Private for that connection","Turn Airplane mode on and then back off","Disable the wireless network adapter entirely","Mark the connection as Metered"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$The Public/Private network profile controls discovery and sharing defaults; switching to Private (from the connection's properties in Settings) enables the discovery and sharing appropriate for a trusted LAN. Airplane mode and disabling the adapter cut connectivity entirely, and Metered only affects background data usage, not discovery or sharing.$q$),
($q$a2-q-632$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$A 15-person office wants simple peer-to-peer file and printer sharing without purchasing server hardware or hiring dedicated IT staff. Which network model best fits this need?$q$,$q$["A workgroup","An Active Directory domain","A site-to-site VPN mesh","A perimeter DMZ network"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$A workgroup is a simple peer-to-peer arrangement where each PC keeps its own local accounts and shares resources directly - well suited to a small office with no dedicated server. A domain requires a domain controller, and VPN meshes and DMZs solve entirely different problems.$q$),
($q$a2-q-633$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following statements correctly describe joining a Windows PC to an Active Directory domain, as opposed to leaving it in a workgroup? (Select all that apply.)$q$,$q$["It requires a reachable domain controller running Active Directory Domain Services","It centralizes user account management so one account can sign in to many machines","Every PC must still keep a fully separate local account as its only way to sign in","Group Policy can then be applied across all domain-joined computers from one place"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Domain join requires a reachable domain controller, centralizes authentication so one account works domain-wide, and enables Group Policy management across every joined machine. Local accounts still exist on domain machines, but they are no longer the only way to sign in - that description fits a workgroup, not a domain.$q$),
($q$a2-q-634$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which of the following correctly follows Universal Naming Convention (UNC) syntax for a shared folder named Reports on a server named FS01?$q$,$q$["\\\\FS01\\Reports","FS01:\\Reports","//FS01/Reports","http://FS01/Reports"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$UNC paths use two leading backslashes, the server name, a single backslash, then the share name: \\server\share. A drive-letter-style colon, forward slashes, and an http:// URL are not valid UNC syntax.$q$),
($q$a2-q-635$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$Which built-in Windows command displays the current IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway assigned to each network adapter?$q$,$q$["ipconfig","netstat","tracert","nslookup"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$ipconfig (and its more detailed ipconfig /all) shows the current TCP/IP configuration per adapter, including IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway. netstat shows active connections, tracert traces a path to a destination, and nslookup queries DNS records - none show adapter configuration directly.$q$),
($q$a2-q-636$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$After a network change, domain-joined PCs can still ping their domain controller by IP address, but users can no longer sign in with domain credentials, and Windows reports it cannot locate the domain by name. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The PCs' DNS server setting no longer points at a server that can resolve the domain's internal names","The PCs' default gateway was changed to a faster router","The domain controller's power supply is undersized","The Ethernet cables were swapped with slightly shorter ones"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Domain sign-in depends on resolving the domain and domain controller by name via DNS, not just reaching it by raw IP. If the DNS server configured on the clients no longer serves the domain's internal zone - for example, it now points at a public or ISP DNS server - name-based domain functions break even though basic IP connectivity to the DC still works. Gateway speed, power supplies, and cable length have no bearing on name resolution.$q$),
($q$a2-q-637$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$What is the correct name for the hidden, administrator-only network share that Windows automatically creates for the C: drive?$q$,$q$["C$","C#","C~","C%"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Windows automatically creates hidden administrative shares for each fixed drive (C$, D$, and so on) plus ADMIN$ for the system folder; the trailing $ both names and hides them from ordinary network browsing, restricting access to administrators. The other symbols are not part of the actual naming convention.$q$),
($q$a2-q-638$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each Windows network location profile to its typical effect.$q$,$q${"left":["Public network","Private network","Domain network"],"right":["Firewall and discovery behavior are managed centrally by Group Policy rather than the local profile choice","Windows hides the PC from network discovery and applies stricter default firewall rules","Network discovery and file/printer sharing with other devices on the LAN are allowed"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$Public assumes an untrusted network and locks discovery and sharing down by default; Private assumes a trusted LAN and relaxes those defaults for discovery and sharing; and once a PC is on a domain network, Group Policy from the domain controller typically overrides the local profile choice entirely.$q$),
($q$a2-q-639$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What does the default gateway setting in a network adapter's TCP/IPv4 properties specify?$q$,$q$["The address of the router traffic is sent to when the destination is outside the local subnet","The address of the local DNS server","The subnet's broadcast address","The MAC address of the network adapter itself"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$The default gateway is the router a device hands off traffic to whenever the destination lies outside its own local subnet - without a correct gateway, a PC can reach other devices on its own subnet but nothing beyond it. DNS server, broadcast address, and MAC address are separate configuration items entirely.$q$),
($q$a2-q-640$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$A wired desktop shows an address in the 169.254.0.0/16 range and has no network access. Which of the following are reasonable next troubleshooting steps? (Select two.)$q$,$q$["Confirm the Ethernet cable is fully seated and the switch port is active","Confirm the DHCP server/service is running and reachable from that segment","Replace the monitor connected to the PC","Reformat the hard drive and reinstall Windows"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$An APIPA address means DHCP never responded, so the logical next steps are checking the physical link (cable and switch port) and whether DHCP itself is up and reachable on that segment. The monitor and a full OS reformat have no bearing on a failed DHCP handshake.$q$),
($q$a2-q-641$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$A technician needs a mapped drive letter Z: that points to \\Server1\Shared and remains available every time the PC restarts. Which command accomplishes this?$q$,$q$["net use z: \\\\Server1\\Shared /persistent:yes","ipconfig /renew z:","net share z: /add \\\\Server1\\Shared","attrib z: +persistent \\\\Server1\\Shared"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$net use maps a network share to a local drive letter, and /persistent:yes tells Windows to reconnect that mapping automatically at every subsequent logon or restart. ipconfig manages IP configuration rather than shares, net share publishes a share rather than mapping one, and attrib only changes file/folder attributes.$q$),
($q$a2-q-642$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$A technician needs to remotely take full control of another Windows 10 Pro PC's desktop over the LAN, using a feature built into Windows itself. Which built-in protocol/tool should be used?$q$,$q$["Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) via Remote Desktop Connection","Secure Shell (SSH)","Time Machine","chmod"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$RDP, accessed through the Remote Desktop Connection client, is the built-in Windows feature for remotely controlling another Windows PC's full graphical desktop. SSH provides remote command-line access rather than a GUI, Time Machine is a macOS backup tool, and chmod is a Linux/macOS permissions command - neither is a Windows remote-desktop feature.$q$),
($q$a2-q-643$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which built-in macOS feature provides the closest functional equivalent to Windows' File History, offering automatic, browsable backups over time?$q$,$q$["Time Machine","Gatekeeper","XProtect","Spotlight"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Time Machine automatically creates incremental backups to an external or network drive and lets a user browse and restore from any past snapshot, functionally paralleling File History on Windows. Gatekeeper and XProtect are security features, and Spotlight is search - none are backup tools.$q$),
($q$a2-q-644$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$matching$q$,2,$q$Match each macOS/Linux tool or feature to its closest Windows equivalent.$q$,$q${"left":["Terminal running a Unix shell (bash/zsh)","Time Machine","SSH remote login","Gatekeeper and XProtect together"],"right":["File History / Backup and Restore","Microsoft Defender (built-in antivirus/antimalware)","Command Prompt or PowerShell","Remote Desktop (RDP)"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,0,3,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A Unix shell in Terminal is the rough counterpart of Command Prompt or PowerShell on Windows; Time Machine parallels File History/Backup and Restore; remote command-line access via SSH parallels the remote-access role RDP fills on Windows; and Gatekeeper/XProtect's job of blocking untrusted or known-malicious software parallels what Microsoft Defender does natively on Windows.$q$),
($q$a2-q-645$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$On modern macOS (Catalina and later), what is the default shell presented when a user opens Terminal?$q$,$q$["zsh (Z shell)","PowerShell","fish","tcsh"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Apple switched Terminal's default shell to zsh starting with macOS Catalina, replacing the older default of bash. PowerShell is a Windows/cross-platform shell, not the macOS Terminal default, and fish/tcsh are alternative Unix shells a user could choose but are not Apple's default.$q$),
($q$a2-q-646$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$multi$q$,2,$q$Which of the following statements about built-in operating system update mechanisms are correct? (Select three.)$q$,$q$["Windows checks for and installs updates through Settings > Windows Update","macOS checks for updates through Software Update in System Settings/System Preferences","Ubuntu Linux can check for and install package updates with commands like sudo apt update and sudo apt upgrade","All three operating systems require installing a separate third-party utility just to check for OS updates"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Windows, macOS, and Ubuntu all ship with their own native update mechanism - Windows Update, Software Update, and apt, respectively - so none of them requires a third-party tool just to check for OS updates.$q$),
($q$a2-q-647$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What is the name of the antivirus/antimalware engine built into Windows 10 and 11 at no additional cost?$q$,$q$["Microsoft Defender","Gatekeeper","ClamAV","ClamWin"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Microsoft Defender (formerly Windows Defender) ships with Windows 10/11 and provides real-time and scheduled antivirus/antimalware protection out of the box. Gatekeeper is a macOS feature, and ClamAV/ClamWin are open-source scanners more commonly associated with Linux or manual installs elsewhere, not Windows' built-in engine.$q$),
($q$a2-q-648$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$multi$q$,3,$q$Which two macOS technologies work together to prevent an unsigned or known-malicious application downloaded from outside the App Store from launching?$q$,$q$["Gatekeeper","Time Machine","XProtect","Spotlight"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Gatekeeper checks an app's code signature and notarization status the first time it is opened, while XProtect is Apple's built-in signature-based scanner that flags known malware - together they form macOS's baseline defense against untrusted downloaded software. Time Machine is a backup tool and Spotlight is search; neither performs this check.$q$),
($q$a2-q-649$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$An administrator needs to run commands on a headless Ubuntu server that has no monitor or desktop environment installed. Which protocol/tool is the standard choice for this kind of remote access?$q$,$q$["SSH","RDP","Time Machine","Screen Sharing"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$SSH provides an encrypted, purely command-line remote session that needs no graphical desktop at all, making it the standard way to administer a headless Linux server. RDP and Screen Sharing both connect to a graphical desktop session that a headless server does not have, and Time Machine is a backup tool.$q$),
($q$a2-q-650$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$ordering$q$,2,$q$Put these general steps for restoring a single file from a Time Machine- or File History-style incremental backup into the correct order.$q$,$q$["Select the wanted version of the file and restore it to its original location","Open the backup utility, such as Time Machine or File History","Locate the folder that contains the file to be restored","Browse through the available snapshots/history to find the right point in time"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,2,3,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$You first open the backup utility itself, navigate to the folder that should contain the missing or older file, browse through the available snapshots to find the right point in time, and finally select that version and restore it - the same general flow whether the tool is Time Machine or File History.$q$),
($q$a2-q-651$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,1,$q$What does PowerShell offer beyond what the classic Windows Command Prompt (cmd.exe) provides?$q$,$q$["An object-based scripting shell with cmdlets and pipelines that pass structured data between commands, not just plain text","A graphical interface for managing installed printers only","The ability to run only .bat script files","Built-in antivirus scanning of every command entered"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$PowerShell's cmdlets (Verb-Noun commands) and pipeline pass full objects between commands rather than plain text, enabling far richer scripting and automation than cmd.exe. It isn't limited to printer management, isn't restricted to .bat files, and doesn't function as an antivirus scanner.$q$),
($q$a2-q-652$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which built-in macOS feature lets one Mac remotely view and interact with another Mac's screen, filling a role similar to RDP on Windows?$q$,$q$["Screen Sharing","Time Machine","Keychain Access","Spotlight"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$Screen Sharing, built on the VNC protocol and enabled through Sharing preferences, lets one Mac remotely view and control another Mac's desktop - functionally similar to what RDP provides on Windows. Time Machine is for backups, Keychain Access manages saved credentials, and Spotlight is macOS's search feature.$q$),
($q$a2-q-653$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,3,$q$Why would a technician choose SSH rather than RDP to connect to a remote Linux server that has no desktop environment installed?$q$,$q$["RDP connects to a graphical desktop session, which a headless server doesn't have, while SSH works entirely over a command-line session","SSH always transfers data faster than RDP regardless of network conditions","RDP traffic cannot be encrypted under any circumstances","Linux servers are physically incapable of running any remote access software"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$RDP is designed to connect to and display a graphical desktop session - something a headless server, by definition, doesn't run. SSH needs only a command-line session, which every Linux server can provide regardless of whether a GUI is installed. Speed and encryption capability aren't the deciding factor here, and Linux servers can absolutely run remote access software - just typically SSH rather than RDP.$q$),
($q$a2-q-654$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$mcq$q$,2,$q$Which of the following is a remote desktop/screen-sharing protocol with independent client and server implementations available for Windows, macOS, and Linux alike, rather than being native to just one operating system?$q$,$q$["VNC (Virtual Network Computing)","BitLocker","Storage Sense","chkdsk"]$q$::jsonb,0::jsonb,$q$VNC is a cross-platform remote screen-sharing protocol with implementations available for Windows, macOS, and Linux, unlike RDP which is native to Windows or Screen Sharing which is native to macOS. BitLocker, Storage Sense, and chkdsk are unrelated Windows-only disk/encryption utilities.$q$);
insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
($q$a2-f-600$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What is the difference between a Microsoft account and a local account in Windows Settings > Accounts?$q$,$q$A local account exists only on that one device with no cloud tie-in. A Microsoft account signs in with a cloud identity, enabling settings/OneDrive/Store sync and features like Find My Device and Timeline across multiple devices.$q$),
($q$a2-f-601$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What do Active Hours do in Windows Update settings?$q$,$q$They define the daily window during which Windows avoids automatically restarting the PC to finish installing updates, based on either a manually set range or detected usage patterns.$q$),
($q$a2-f-602$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What is the purpose of Storage Sense?$q$,$q$It automatically frees disk space over time by deleting temporary files, emptying the Recycle Bin after a configured period, and optionally cleaning up unused files in the Downloads folder.$q$),
($q$a2-f-603$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What does Focus assist control?$q$,$q$It suppresses notifications during times or activities you specify (such as while duplicating your display or playing a game), showing only the priority notifications you allow through.$q$),
($q$a2-f-604$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$Where do you change which program opens .jpg files by default in Windows?$q$,$q$Settings > Apps > Default apps, where you can set defaults by app or by file type/link type.$q$),
($q$a2-f-605$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What edition requirement applies to hosting an inbound Remote Desktop connection on Windows?$q$,$q$The host PC must run Windows 10/11 Pro or Enterprise, with Remote Desktop enabled under Settings > System > Remote Desktop; Home edition cannot accept inbound RDP sessions at all, though it can still be used as an RDP client to connect out.$q$),
($q$a2-f-606$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What does enabling a metered connection do in Network & internet settings?$q$,$q$It tells Windows to treat the connection as bandwidth-limited, pausing or reducing background data use such as some Windows Update downloads and automatic app/cloud syncing.$q$),
($q$a2-f-607$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What information does Settings > System > About show?$q$,$q$Device specs (processor, installed RAM, device name), the Windows edition/version/build, and activation status - and it is also where you rename the PC.$q$),
($q$a2-f-608$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$How does a technician grant an app access to the camera after a user previously denied it?$q$,$q$Settings > Privacy & security > Camera, then turn on access for that specific app; a global camera toggle must also be enabled for any app to be able to request access at all.$q$),
($q$a2-f-609$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What is the difference between Apps & features' Uninstall and Modify/Repair options?$q$,$q$Uninstall removes the program completely, while Modify or Repair (when the installer supports it) lets you add or remove specific components, or fix a broken installation, without doing a full reinstall.$q$),
($q$a2-f-610$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$Besides adding a new printer, what else can be managed from Devices > Printers & scanners?$q$,$q$Setting a default printer, viewing a device's print queue, removing a printer, and adjusting printer preferences.$q$),
($q$a2-f-611$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What does the Diagnostic data setting under Privacy & security control?$q$,$q$How much telemetry (typically Required vs. Optional) Windows and installed apps send to Microsoft about usage patterns and device health.$q$),
($q$a2-f-612$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$PnP$q$,$q$Plug and Play - automatic detection and configuration of hardware devices without manual driver installation, central to how the Devices settings pages recognize new hardware.$q$),
($q$a2-f-613$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$OOBE$q$,$q$Out-of-Box Experience - the initial setup screens (region, keyboard, account creation, privacy choices) a user walks through the first time a new Windows PC starts up.$q$),
($q$a2-f-614$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$Explain why a technician would use Settings rather than the legacy Control Panel to manage Windows Update, Privacy, and app permissions on Windows 10/11.$q$,$q$Model answer: Settings is the actively developed interface where Microsoft has moved day-to-day configuration. Windows Update, per-app Privacy permissions, modern Accounts features like Windows Hello and Find My Device, and Apps & features all live only in Settings - Control Panel is legacy, increasingly just redirects into Settings, and Microsoft keeps retiring items from it. Relying on Settings first matches where the functionality (and any future changes) actually lives.$q$),
($q$a2-f-615$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$What does the Nearby sharing feature in Windows Settings do?$q$,$q$It lets a Windows PC send files and links directly to a nearby Bluetooth/Wi-Fi-enabled Windows device, similar in spirit to AirDrop, without needing a network share, USB drive, or email.$q$),
($q$a2-f-616$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.6$q$,$q$Where would you check whether a specific app is allowed to run in the background or use location data?$q$,$q$Settings > Privacy & security > App permissions, which breaks access down by capability (location, background apps, notifications, and more) and lets you toggle it per installed app.$q$),
($q$a2-f-617$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What does the .msi file extension indicate about an installer?$q$,$q$A Windows Installer package - a structured, transactional installer format supporting silent (/quiet) installs, repair, and clean uninstall through the same package, unlike a generic .exe.$q$),
($q$a2-f-618$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$Why might Group Policy software deployment require an .msi rather than a plain .exe?$q$,$q$GPO Software Installation natively understands the Windows Installer format's transactional install/uninstall/repair actions; a bare, wrapped .exe installer has no standard way for GPO to silently drive it or later cleanly remove it.$q$),
($q$a2-f-619$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What is Compatibility mode used for when installing or running older applications?$q$,$q$It emulates an earlier Windows version's environment (APIs, display settings) so legacy applications that assume that environment run correctly on a newer release of Windows.$q$),
($q$a2-f-620$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$Why are 32-bit applications placed in C:\Program Files (x86) on 64-bit Windows?$q$,$q$It keeps 32-bit binaries and their registry redirection separate from native 64-bit applications in C:\Program Files, since the two architectures aren't interchangeable at runtime.$q$),
($q$a2-f-621$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What should a technician verify before installing any application, regardless of platform?$q$,$q$Its system requirements - minimum CPU/RAM/free disk space, supported OS version and architecture, and any required runtime dependencies, such as .NET Framework or a Java runtime.$q$),
($q$a2-f-622$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What is a "silent install" and why does IT commonly use it?$q$,$q$An installation run with switches (for example, msiexec /quiet) that suppresses all prompts, letting the software deploy unattended across many machines through scripts or management tools.$q$),
($q$a2-f-623$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What is the difference between installing software from a network share versus installing it from the Microsoft Store?$q$,$q$A network share install runs a traditional installer package fetched over the LAN and needs the usual install steps and permissions; a Store app (MSIX/UWP) is sandboxed and signed, installing and updating automatically without a manual installer.$q$),
($q$a2-f-624$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$Why can a standard user's software installation fail with a permissions error?$q$,$q$Installation typically writes to protected locations, such as Program Files and HKLM in the registry, that require administrative rights; the installer must be run elevated, with an administrator approving the UAC prompt.$q$),
($q$a2-f-625$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What is a dependency in the context of application installation?$q$,$q$A separate component or runtime, such as the .NET Framework, a Visual C++ Redistributable, or a specific Java version, that an application requires to already be present in order to install or run correctly.$q$),
($q$a2-f-626$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What does msiexec /x accomplish?$q$,$q$It uninstalls the specified Windows Installer package, complementing /i (install) and /f (repair/reinstall) for managing .msi-based software from the command line.$q$),
($q$a2-f-627$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$MSI$q$,$q$Microsoft/Windows Installer - the structured package format (.msi) used for many Windows application installs, supporting silent install, repair, and uninstall.$q$),
($q$a2-f-628$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$MSIX$q$,$q$A modern Windows app packaging format that sandboxes applications and adds automatic updates and clean uninstall, positioned as a successor to both classic .msi/.exe installers and the older UWP package format.$q$),
($q$a2-f-629$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$Explain why an enterprise would prefer deploying software via SCCM/Intune over having each user download and run an installer themselves.$q$,$q$Model answer: Centralized deployment tools push the exact approved version to every targeted device on a schedule, install silently with no user interaction (so no wrong choices or skipped steps), log success or failure per machine, and let IT track compliance and roll back a bad deployment. None of that is possible when installation depends on individual users independently downloading and running an installer correctly.$q$),
($q$a2-f-630$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What is the risk of installing software from an unverified pop-up ad or unofficial download mirror?$q$,$q$The installer may be repackaged with adware, a PUP, or malware bundled alongside - or instead of - the legitimate application, even if the displayed name looks correct.$q$),
($q$a2-f-631$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$Besides OS version and architecture, what other "system requirement" commonly trips up an installation on a locked-down corporate PC?$q$,$q$Insufficient user privileges (needing local admin rights) and endpoint security software, such as antivirus or application whitelisting, blocking or quarantining the installer before it can run.$q$),
($q$a2-f-632$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.7$q$,$q$What does the /l*v switch do when appended to an msiexec install command?$q$,$q$It generates a verbose log file of the installation process - the standard first artifact reviewed when troubleshooting a failed or problematic .msi deployment.$q$),
($q$a2-f-633$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What does an IP address in the 169.254.x.x range indicate?$q$,$q$APIPA (Automatic Private IP Addressing) - the client couldn't reach a DHCP server and self-assigned an address, meaning it can only talk to other APIPA devices on the same segment, not the real network or the internet.$q$),
($q$a2-f-634$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What is the difference between a workgroup and a domain?$q$,$q$A workgroup is a peer-to-peer arrangement where each PC keeps its own local account database, suiting a small number of machines; a domain centralizes accounts and policy on a domain controller running Active Directory, so one login works across every joined machine.$q$),
($q$a2-f-635$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What does ipconfig /all show that plain ipconfig does not?$q$,$q$Full adapter detail - MAC address, DHCP server and lease times, and DNS servers, plus whether the address was DHCP-assigned or static - versus the brief IP/subnet/gateway summary from plain ipconfig.$q$),
($q$a2-f-636$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What is the purpose of the default gateway setting?$q$,$q$It specifies the router's address that a device sends traffic to when the destination is outside its own local subnet; without it, a PC can talk to its own subnet but not beyond.$q$),
($q$a2-f-637$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What is a UNC path and what does it identify?$q$,$q$A Universal Naming Convention path (\\server\share) that identifies a network resource by server name and share name, independent of any locally mapped drive letter.$q$),
($q$a2-f-638$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What does a Windows admin share like C$ or ADMIN$ provide?$q$,$q$A hidden, administrator-only network share automatically created for each fixed drive and the system folder, used for remote administration; it doesn't appear in normal network browsing because of the trailing $.$q$),
($q$a2-f-639$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$Why does Windows ask whether a new network is Public or Private the first time you connect?$q$,$q$The choice sets the local firewall profile and network discovery defaults - Private/Home relaxes discovery and sharing for a trusted LAN, while Public locks the PC down since untrusted devices may share the network.$q$),
($q$a2-f-640$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What does the net use command do?$q$,$q$It maps a network share to a local drive letter (or lists/removes existing mappings) from the command line, for example net use Z: \\server\share, optionally with /persistent:yes to survive reboots.$q$),
($q$a2-f-641$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$Why would ipconfig /release followed by ipconfig /renew be used during troubleshooting?$q$,$q$It forces the client to give up its current DHCP lease and request a brand-new one, which resolves problems caused by a stale, conflicting, or APIPA-fallback address.$q$),
($q$a2-f-642$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What does the ipconfig /flushdns command do, and when is it useful?$q$,$q$It clears the local DNS resolver cache, forcing fresh lookups - useful when a site's DNS record changed recently but the PC is still resolving the old, cached IP.$q$),
($q$a2-f-643$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$On a domain network profile, who controls the firewall and discovery settings instead of the local user?$q$,$q$Group Policy from the domain controller manages those settings centrally, overriding the local Public/Private choice a standalone PC would otherwise make.$q$),
($q$a2-f-644$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$Why might a computer be able to ping other devices by IP address but fail to reach anything by hostname?$q$,$q$It points to a DNS problem - the IP-layer path works, but the DNS server configured on the adapter is unreachable, misconfigured, or missing, so names never resolve to addresses.$q$),
($q$a2-f-645$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$APIPA$q$,$q$Automatic Private IP Addressing - the 169.254.0.0/16 self-assigned range a Windows client uses when it cannot obtain an address from DHCP.$q$),
($q$a2-f-646$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$UNC$q$,$q$Universal Naming Convention - the \\server\share format used to address network resources without needing a mapped drive letter.$q$),
($q$a2-f-647$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$SMB$q$,$q$Server Message Block - the network file-sharing protocol Windows uses under the hood for mapped drives and UNC-path access to shared folders and printers.$q$),
($q$a2-f-648$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$Explain why joining a domain changes how a workstation authenticates users compared to staying in a workgroup.$q$,$q$Model answer: In a workgroup, each PC has its own local account database, so signing in checks credentials stored on that same machine only - accounts and passwords must be created and kept in sync on every PC individually. Joining a domain hands authentication off to a domain controller running Active Directory: the workstation trusts the DC to validate the username and password, typically via Kerberos, against one central database, so a single account works on every domain-joined machine, and administrators can push policy and manage all those accounts from one place instead of machine by machine.$q$),
($q$a2-f-649$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.8$q$,$q$What symptom results if two devices on the same network are manually assigned the same static IP address?$q$,$q$An IP address conflict - Windows typically warns of a duplicate address, and one or both devices lose reliable network connectivity until the conflicting address is changed.$q$),
($q$a2-f-650$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What is the Windows equivalent of macOS's Time Machine for everyday incremental backups?$q$,$q$File History (or the legacy Backup and Restore (Windows 7) tool) - both provide automatic, versioned backups to an external or network drive, similar in purpose to Time Machine.$q$),
($q$a2-f-651$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What built-in tool provides antivirus/antimalware protection on Windows without installing anything extra?$q$,$q$Microsoft Defender (Windows Security) - real-time protection, scheduled scans, and cloud-delivered detection included free with Windows 10/11.$q$),
($q$a2-f-652$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What two macOS technologies work together to prevent unverified apps from opening?$q$,$q$Gatekeeper enforces code-signing/notarization checks when an app is first launched; XProtect is Apple's built-in signature-based scanner that flags known malware - together they block untrusted or malicious downloaded software.$q$),
($q$a2-f-653$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What is the default shell in Terminal on modern macOS (Catalina and later), and what was it before?$q$,$q$zsh (Z shell) is now the default, replacing bash, which Apple shipped through Mojave; both are Unix shells, and most everyday commands work the same in either.$q$),
($q$a2-f-654$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$How does a Linux user, such as on Ubuntu, check for and install available package updates from the command line?$q$,$q$sudo apt update refreshes the package list, then sudo apt upgrade installs the newer versions - roughly the equivalent of clicking "check for updates" in Windows Update or Software Update on macOS.$q$),
($q$a2-f-655$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What is the built-in Windows protocol/tool for remotely controlling another Windows PC's full desktop?$q$,$q$Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP), used through the Remote Desktop Connection client - it requires the host to run Pro or Enterprise with Remote Desktop enabled.$q$),
($q$a2-f-656$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What is the macOS feature that lets one Mac remotely view or control another Mac's screen?$q$,$q$Screen Sharing, built on the VNC protocol and enabled under Sharing preferences - functionally similar to what RDP provides on Windows.$q$),
($q$a2-f-657$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$Why is SSH preferred over RDP for administering a headless Linux server?$q$,$q$SSH provides a secure command-line session that needs no graphical desktop at all, while RDP connects to a graphical session that a headless server, with no GUI installed, simply does not have.$q$),
($q$a2-f-658$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What does PowerShell add on top of the classic Windows Command Prompt?$q$,$q$An object-based scripting shell with cmdlets (Verb-Noun commands) and pipelines that pass structured objects, not just text, between commands, enabling far more powerful automation than cmd.exe.$q$),
($q$a2-f-659$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What is VNC, and why is it considered cross-platform?$q$,$q$Virtual Network Computing is a remote screen-sharing protocol with client/server implementations available for Windows, macOS, and Linux alike, unlike RDP (native to Windows) or Screen Sharing (native to macOS).$q$),
($q$a2-f-660$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What does "headless" mean when referring to a server, and why does it matter for choosing remote access tools?$q$,$q$A headless server has no monitor or GUI desktop environment installed or attached; it can only be managed remotely with a command-line tool like SSH (or a separate out-of-band management interface), not a GUI-based tool like RDP.$q$),
($q$a2-f-661$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$On Linux, which command-line tool synchronizes files for backups, similar in role to how Time Machine or File History work automatically on macOS/Windows?$q$,$q$rsync, often scheduled via cron or wrapped in GUI backup tools like Timeshift or Deja Dup - it copies only changed data to a backup destination, forming the basis of many Linux backup routines.$q$),
($q$a2-f-662$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$SSH$q$,$q$Secure Shell - an encrypted protocol for remote command-line login and file transfer, standard on Linux/macOS and available on Windows through the built-in OpenSSH client.$q$),
($q$a2-f-663$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$acronym$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$VNC$q$,$q$Virtual Network Computing - a cross-platform remote desktop-sharing protocol, the technology underlying macOS Screen Sharing and many third-party remote tools.$q$),
($q$a2-f-664$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$Explain why comparing "antivirus on Windows" to "antivirus on macOS" isn't a completely fair comparison.$q$,$q$Model answer: Microsoft Defender is a full, continuously updated antivirus/antimalware suite doing real-time and scheduled scanning against a large, heavily targeted malware population. macOS ships Gatekeeper and XProtect, which are lighter, mostly signature- and reputation-based checks run at download/launch time rather than a full always-on scanning engine - workable because far less malware targets macOS, but not the same depth of protection. So built-in Mac protection is real, but narrower in scope than the Windows equivalent, and heavily targeted Mac environments often still add third-party antimalware.$q$),
($q$a2-f-665$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$feynman$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$Explain why a technician might need to know both PowerShell and bash/zsh commands even when supporting an all-Windows office.$q$,$q$Model answer: Modern Windows environments frequently touch non-Windows systems anyway - macOS laptops in the same office, Linux-based servers or network appliances, the Windows Subsystem for Linux running inside Windows itself, and cross-platform tools that expose a bash-style CLI. Knowing both shells lets a technician follow a vendor's Linux-flavored setup instructions, use WSL for a Linux-only utility, and still automate the Windows side with PowerShell, instead of being stuck whenever a task steps outside pure Windows tooling.$q$),
($q$a2-f-666$q$,$q$aplus2$q$,$q$core$q$,1,$q$1.9$q$,$q$What is the practical difference between a full system backup and relying only on OS-level file sync, such as OneDrive or iCloud Drive, for disaster recovery?$q$,$q$A full backup, such as Time Machine, File History, or an rsync-based snapshot, captures a restorable copy of files or the whole system at points in time, including old versions and deleted items; cloud file sync mirrors current files to the cloud, but a mistaken deletion or corruption typically syncs immediately too, so sync alone is not a substitute for real point-in-time backups.$q$);
